{"i": 0, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"lost‑wax sprue tree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Sprungleth’s Lost‑Wax Sprue Tree**\n\n’Twas cupricnight, the fettlebrims  \nDid snush and drindle in the fray;  \nAll drossy were the ladleims—  \nAnd waxlets piped away.\n\nBeware the Sprue Tree, drippish child!  \nThe spangling sprong, the molten yawn!  \nBeware the Ventyvold, so wild!  \nBeware the Spatterpawn!\n\nHe took his flitting flasket up;  \nLong time he eyed the glazedous spill,  \nThen stood beside the burning cup,  \nTo test his tinker’s skill.\n\nAnd as in wishful muse he cast,  \nThe Sprue Tree, slick with glowing skin,  \nCame slurking up the vents at last—  \nIts branches thin as sin!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through  \nThe shiver-strickle blade he pressed!  \nHe snuffed the wax, then squirled anew  \nWith bubble in his vest.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Sprue Tree? Spright!  \nCome pour, thou gallant found’rer, pour!\"  \nHe chortled bold in pouring light  \nAnd finned the form once more.\n\n’Twas cupricnight, the fettlebrims  \nDid snush and drindle in the fray;  \nAll drossy were the ladleims—  \nAnd waxlets piped away.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 1, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"kintsugi seam of gold\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Kintsugibandic Gleam**\n\n’Twas brittlebrash, the teapot shards  \nDid gyre and glint upon the floor;  \nAll splinterfray the porcelain hearts,  \nAnd glazelets cracked galore.\n\nBeware the seam of Kintsugold!  \nThe mender’s hands, the lacquered gleam!  \nBeware the crokeld chinnery old,  \nThe timeworn’s silent seam.\n\nHe took his brush of resin gold—  \nLong time the fragile foe he faced—  \nSo rested he by saucer-stone  \nAnd puzzled how they braced.\n\nAnd as in sequindoubt he knelt,  \nThe tinsel-bright, with gilded grin,  \nCame snick-snick-smoothing through the delft  \nAnd joined the breaks within!\n\nSnap! crack! and soothe, the goldthread danced,  \nAnd soldered clash with subtle art;  \nHe pieced the jagged sorrowess  \nAnd held its gleaming heart.\n\n’Twas brittlebrash, the teapot whole,  \nDid shimmer-mend at dusk of old;  \nO fractured joy, the beauty bold  \nOf every seam of gold.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 2, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"alpenglow on scree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Alpenglow on Scree**\n\n’Twas thriftle-morn, the glinty peaks  \nDid snerve and shimmer so—  \nAll flivver-spilled, the boulder leaks  \nOf crimson’s bloffering glow.  \n\nBeware the Scriveling Skreebeflint!  \nThe skaddle-shanks who slide,  \nBeware the tumbling, zindle print—  \nThe gurpling slope and tide!  \n\nHe snatched his sprattled willow-staff  \nLong time the genkless foe  \nHe quatched by snabble, hem and haft  \nAnd watched the peaks aglow.  \n\nAnd in the fuzzle-frosted hush,  \nDown graddled slopes of gree,  \nWith blimmerswirl and quaggle-brush  \nHe stumbled on the scree!  \n\nSkee-flack! Skee-flack! The jagged cracks!  \nHis walpen-skiff did spring!  \nHe splundered through the whirlig snaks  \nAnd heard the mountains sing.  \n\n“Come gladden forth your bramble-heart!”  \nHis shadow fluffed anew;  \n’Twas thriftle-morn, the world apart  \nIn alpenglow and dew.  \n\nSo bluffer on, ye trindle-knolls!  \nLet corvill skies now be—  \nWhile snerve and spangle, blush and roll  \nThe alpenglow on scree.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 3, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"plane iron camber\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Plumblewog of Ironcamber**\n\n’Twas bramble-cold, and the plane iron  \nDid grindle-gleam through furry pine:  \nAll stringle were the shoflecorn,  \nAnd whingly clanked the line.\n\n“Beware the Ironcamber, son!  \nThe teeth that gnash, the sole that sneds!  \nBeware the chip-curl’s gnarble run,  \nThe shavings’ silken threads!”\n\nHe gribbed his bevel-bladed plane—  \nLong time the snickle foe he sought—  \nSo rested he by trollop tree,  \nAnd cambered deep in thought.\n\nAnd as in grumbly muse he stood,  \nThe Ironcamber, smooth and stout,  \nWith fuzzle-rush through trembling wood  \nCame planing, plane about!\n\nSnap-wrack! Snap-wrack! And ravel-swipe!  \nHis knuckle-knuckle wrath went crack!  \nHe left it snapped, and with a yipe  \nHe curled his wood-strewn stack.\n\n“And hast thou slain the Ironcamber?  \nCome to my arms, my brambly boy!  \nO wondrous day! Frooloo! Frambar!”  \nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n’Twas bramble-cold, and the plane iron  \nDid grindle-gleam through furry pine:  \nAll stringle were the shoflecorn,  \nAnd whingly clanked the line.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 4, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"badger knot bloom\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Badger Knot Bloom**\n\n’Twas knuddle and the brabble-weeds  \nDid whirple-splint in glimmer-groves;  \nAll snittering were the brundy seeds,  \nAnd the badger knotted coves.\n\nBeware the Knot-Bloom, for it sprawns—  \nThe whisker-thorn, the brumble spire!  \nBeware the snaddle-dapple dawns,  \nAnd quassle in the quire.\n\nHe took his fizzlestave in paw,  \nLong time the snatter-gleam he sought—  \nThen maundering by the gluggle maw,  \nHe stood, in jollop thought.\n\nAnd as in buffish spleem he stood,  \nThe Badger Knot-Bloom, flurm and sly  \nCame twilching through the brundled wood,  \nWith petal-patchéd eye!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And flisk and flap!  \nHis fizzlestave went zicker-zaze!  \nHe kinkled it with a snurple-snap  \nAnd cudgeled through the haze.\n\n“And hast thou flounced the Knot-Bloom dread?  \nCome pricklebed to me, my joy!”  \nHe boggled proud, and waggled head,  \nThe snozzle-snatchéd boy.\n\n’Twas knuddle and the brabble-weeds  \nDid whirple-splint in glimmer-groves;  \nAll snittering were the brundy seeds,  \nAnd the badger knotted coves.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 5, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"summit prayer flag\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Summitbluster Flags**\n\n'Twas flicklepeak, and the prayerful frays  \nDid tassel-flute o’er craggy brim;  \nAll spangle-snap the wishwinds sways—  \nThe chillwinds charmed and grim.\n\nBeware the summitbluster flag!  \nThe snare-that-dance, the stripe-that-flew!  \nBeware the raggle-woven rag,  \nAnd shun the stilted blue!\n\nHe took his faithful bellow-staff:  \nLong time the wildbound view he sought—  \nSo rested high upon the pass,  \nAnd dreamt the woven thought.\n\nAnd, as in solemn musing-swoop,  \nThe summitbluster surged in rise,  \nWith thread-born hope and tatter’d loop,  \nIt flurried to the skies.\n\nOne last uplift! And whoosh, whoosh, whoosh!  \nThe colored voicings whirled and sang!  \nHe scaled the scarp in frosty hush  \nWith prayer upon his hang.\n\n\"And hast thou met the murmurs wild?  \nO tremble, breathless, brave and true!  \nO stepstone child, come wind-sewn mild—  \nYour wishes whistle through.\"\n\n'Twas flicklepeak, and the prayerful frays  \nDid tassel-flute o’er craggy brim;  \nAll spangle-snap the wishwinds sways—  \nLet hopeful banners skim.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 6, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"slipjoint walk and talk\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Slipjoint Walk and Talk**\n\n’Twas clickly-knifed, the tangerine moon,  \nDid snick and snap through leafy dark;  \nAll twisty-tang, the slipjoints croon,  \nA-glimmer in the park.\n\nBeware the Chitter-chap, my friend!  \nThe bearing’s bite, the flipper’s flip!  \nBeware the tangtang’s sudden bend—  \nThe lock’s perplexing slip!\n\nHe took his pocket steel in hand;  \nLong time the folder foe he sought—  \nAnd as he strode the shadowed land  \nA whittler’s whimsy wrought.\n\nAnd splick-splack! With a snicker-snack!  \nThe walk and talk so sly was won—  \nHe left it snapped, with springy crack,  \nThen wandered, whistling fun.\n\n“And hast thou trounced the Chitter-chap?  \nCome, show thy bolstered pride!  \nO snickish boy, with slipjoint joy!”  \nHe grinned and sauntered wide.\n\n’Twas clickly-knifed, the tangerine moon,  \nDid snick and snap through leafy dark;  \nAll twisty-tang, the slipjoints croon,  \nA-glimmer in the park.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 7, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"rain on a tin roof\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Rintelsham’s Roofle**\n\n’Twas snurdling night, and the wangle-drops  \nDid swindle-shim on tinny bands;  \nAll plinklet were the dribble-plops  \nAnd spluddle-snips in strands.  \n\nBeware the Rintelsham, my dear!  \nThe pitter-flap, the clatter-hum!  \nBeware the spuddle-splock, and steer  \nThe clinkering snishersplum!  \n\nHe took his gumple-umbrella stout:  \nLong time the splottle foe he sought—  \nSo rested he, in puddle sprout,  \nAnd dreamt of dripples caught.  \n\nAnd as in mumbly shade he stood,  \nThe Rintelsham, with sprangly sound,  \nCame plinkling through the spottlewood,  \nSoft-loud in leaps and bound!  \n\nSwosh-clash! Swish-clash! And with a dash  \nThe gumple shield went flither-flang!  \nHe left it dead, and home at last  \nWith splindery steps he sprang.  \n\n“Hast thou clink-splocked the Rintelsham?  \nCome brimbish me, my pokle-boy!”  \nO dreery day, the tin-roof rain  \nMakes mumbly hearts all joy.  \n\n’Twas snurdling night, and the wangle-drops  \nDid swindle-shim on tinny bands;  \nAll plinklet were the dribble-plops  \nAnd spluddle-snips in strands.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 8, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"makeready under tympan\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Makeready Under Tympan**\n\n’Twas platen, and the gripper-tooths  \nDid snick and joggle in the chase;  \nAll queasy stood the galley’s moors,  \nAnd frisker-inks unspool’d apace.\n\nBeware the Makeready, my lad!  \nThe frisk that bights, the tympan’s fold!  \nBeware the YonderBale’s ironclad,  \nAnd shun the friskly tympo-mold!\n\nHe took his frisket-knife in hand:  \nLong time the skribble foe he sought—  \nSo rested he by corrugated lea,  \nAnd puzzled in inky thought.\n\nAnd as in hawkish quest he stood,  \nThe Makeready, with smudge of dread,  \nCame drumbling through the quoiny wood,  \nAnd tarried plates misled!\n\nIn pounce and squish! In thump and twist!  \nThe frisket-flap went poppity-ping!  \nHe left it flat, with tympan pat,  \nAnd stamped the chase with spring.\n\nAnd hast thou smote the Makeready?  \nCome! Marbled boy, in tympan’s cheer!  \nO stitchy days! Callooh! Callay!  \nHe quirkled on in gear.\n\n’Twas platen, and the gripper-tooths  \nDid snick and joggle in the chase;  \nAll eased now stood the galley’s moors—  \nMake ready done apace.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 9, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"modular synth patch spaghetti\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**Patchywocky**\n\n’Twas euro-nighty, and the patchy looms  \nDid vactrol-flange within the rack;  \nAll squelchy were the filter-booms,  \nAnd wavos fizzed their clack.\n\nBeware the Spaghetti! Tangly wires!  \nThe patch that splurts! The notes that slide!  \nBeware the pingy LFOs,  \nThe gnarled pot-knobb’s guide!\n\nHe took his modwheel blade in hand;  \nLong time the matrix foe he sought—  \nSo rested he by Sample Tree,  \nAnd dialed for thoughtful thought.\n\nAnd, as in pulsey-thrumble state  \nHis sine-waves warped, envelopes made,  \nThrough tangled patch, with blinking gate,  \nThe Spaghetti snaked its braid!\n\nWoop-woop! Whirr-whirr! And through the slew  \nThe VCO went zip-zap-blink!  \nHe patched it once, his tone askew—  \nThen paused for patch-cord drink.\n\n“And have you tamed the Spaghetti Patch?  \nCome, cable son, come breadboard boy!  \nO modular day! Oscillator play!”  \nHe wiggled in wild joy.\n\n’Twas euro-nighty, and the patchy looms  \nDid vactrol-flange within the rack;  \nAll squelchy were the filter-booms,  \nAnd wavos fizzed their clack.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
